{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Two omers.</b> And all the days of the year they would make two loaves from one omer; if so, on the sixth day they would make from two omers — for one, four loaves. And on the sixth day they would eat one of them and three remained, and they would have to make a double portion for the evening, at the onset of Shabbat — they eat one, for their way is to eat bread at every meal — and two loaves remained for Shabbat, and they had lechem mishneh. And this is a proof that one does not break bread on two loaves at the Sabbath afternoon meal. However, I saw my master Marinon who would break bread on two loaves at the Sabbath afternoon meal, and I received from my master Abraham a proof from what is stated there: ״ראו כי ה׳ נתן לכם השבת״ etc. — Rabbeinu Yitzchak said: if the nations of the world say to you, why do you keep the Sabbath? Answer them: See the miracle — every day one portion of manna would descend for Israel, and on the Sabbath two portions. That is to say, doubled — that is, every meal that descends for the sake of Shabbat descends doubled in its bread. And so they would do on the sixth day: in the morning they would make from half an omer one loaf, and when they came to bake the omer — and this is 'from two omers for one' — for the sake of Shabbat every half-omer doubled into two loaves. You thus have six loaves from an omer and a half. And in all three meals they would eat two loaves, for it is the way to eat more on Shabbat than on weekday meals, because of the additional soul. And according to this you will find that on the afternoon meal of Shabbat they had two loaves, and therefore one should be accustomed to break bread on two loaves also at the afternoon meal. But there is no kiddush, for even on Shabbat morning they do not recite kiddush unless it is more than on the day of Shabbat itself, as is concluded in tractate Beitza."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The phrase משני העומר לאחד (literally 'from two omers to one') is read here as referring to the ratio of manna-to-loaves: two omers yield four loaves on a weekday basis, hence 'for one [omer, two loaves].' The translation preserves the compilers' own reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:03:24.243797615Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}